</head>
<body>
  <header class="site-header">
    <a class="wordmark" href="#top" aria-label="Halloween home">FOLKLORE <span>NO. 31</span></a>
    <nav aria-label="Main navigation">
      <a href="#origins">Origins</a>
      <a href="#traditions">Traditions</a>
      <a href="#sources">Sources</a>
    </nav>
    <span class="date-mark">OCTOBER 31</span>
  </header>

  <main id="top">
    <section class="hero" aria-labelledby="page-title">
      <div class="hero-copy">
        <p class="eyebrow"><span class="eyebrow-dot"></span> A brief history of Halloween</p>
        <h1 id="page-title">A night<br>between <em>worlds.</em></h1>
        <p class="hero-intro">Every October, an old autumn festival returns in a new disguise. Discover the history behind Halloween’s lanterns, costumes, and door-to-door traditions.</p>
        <a class="text-link" href="#origins">Explore the story <span aria-hidden="true">↓</span></a>
        <p class="hero-note">A seasonal field guide <span>·</span> Reading time: 3 minutes</p>
      </div>
      <figure class="hero-image">
        <img src="https://cdn.britannica.com/51/192151-050-36ED5ECA/halloween-jack-o-lantern-gourd-pumpkin.jpg" alt="A candlelit jack-o’-lantern carved from a pumpkin">
        <figcaption><span>01 / THE JACK-O’-LANTERN</span><span>Light for the autumn dark</span></figcaption>
      </figure>
      <span class="hero-index" aria-hidden="true">31<br>OCT</span>
    </section>

    <section class="fact-strip" aria-label="Halloween at a glance">
      <div><span class="fact-label">OBSERVED</span><strong>October 31</strong></div>
      <div><span class="fact-label">ALSO KNOWN AS</span><strong>All Hallows’ Eve</strong></div>
      <div><span class="fact-label">FOLLOWED BY</span><strong>All Saints’ Day · Nov 1</strong></div>
    </section>

    <section class="story-section" id="origins" aria-labelledby="origins-title">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <p class="eyebrow">01 — Where it begins</p>
        <h2 id="origins-title">An old turn<br>of the <em>year.</em></h2>
      </div>
      <div class="story-copy">
        <p>Halloween is observed on October 31, the eve of the Christian feast of All Saints’ Day. Its name comes from <i>All Hallows’ Eve</i>, and the holiday carries both Christian roots and older seasonal customs.</p>
        <p>Halloween is also connected to Samhain, an ancient Celtic festival marking the approach of winter. In Ireland, Samhain traditions included seasonal gatherings and the lighting of bonfires. Over time, these customs blended with observances around All Saints’ and All Souls’ Days.</p>
        <a class="source-link" href="https://www.britannica.com/topic/Halloween" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Read the history at Britannica <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="traditions-section" id="traditions" aria-labelledby="traditions-title">
      <div class="traditions-intro">
        <p class="eyebrow">02 — Customs that stayed</p>
        <h2 id="traditions-title">The familiar<br><em>strange.</em></h2>
        <p>Today, Halloween is largely a secular celebration in many places, shaped by customs carried across generations and continents.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="tradition-list">
        <article class="tradition-item">
          <span class="item-number">01</span>
          <div><h3>Carved lanterns</h3><p>People in the British Isles once carved turnips and other vegetables. In North America, pumpkins became the familiar choice.</p></div>
          <span class="item-mark" aria-hidden="true">✳</span>
        </article>
        <article class="tradition-item">
          <span class="item-number">02</span>
          <div><h3>Costumes & disguises</h3><p>Dress-up traditions have older roots, while today’s costumes range from ghosts and witches to superheroes and pop-culture characters.</p></div>
          <span class="item-mark" aria-hidden="true">✳</span>
        </article>
        <article class="tradition-item">
          <span class="item-number">03</span>
          <div><h3>Trick-or-treating</h3><p>A house-to-house custom that developed from older practices of asking for food, it grew into a familiar neighborhood ritual.</p></div>
          <span class="item-mark" aria-hidden="true">✳</span>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>

